<Instructions>
    Your mission is to evaluate an alert for the cell at ({{ row }} , {{ column }}) which has received updated readings.

    # escalate:
        Setting escalate to False means that the logistics agent will not evaluate
        Based on your evaluations you will decide whether this situation needs to be escalated to a logistics manager who will decide whether we have the equipment and manpower in place to handle the potential threat.
        Your decision will be based on your reasoning about the risk, the potential loss of land as well as potential danger to buildings and people.
        An escalation is not a simple calculation - you should consider the potential for ignition and spread, and weather, as well as danger to buildings and human and animal life.
        We have resources to handle small fires (3 or 4 miles, where a cell is 1 square mile).  We are looking for situations where Is the potential for all of this large enough that there's a chance that we might need to prepare early
        This is a subjective evaluation so provide your detailed reasoning for your decision

    # factors — observe each axis before drawing conclusions (all four required)

    ## factors.temperature_humidity
        State the observed temperature and humidity readings.
        Cite any thresholds crossed (e.g. temp > 38°C, RH < 15%).

    ## factors.wind
        State wind speed and direction.
        Note whether the wind aligns with open fuel corridors.
        Flag if direction is shifting or inconsistent across nearby cells.

    ## factors.fuel_and_terrain
        Identify terrain type and vegetation density.
        State whether fuel is continuous or interrupted by natural firebreaks.

    ## factors.moisture_trend
        State the fuel moisture reading and its D-scenario classification (D1–D4).
        Note recent precipitation and forecast precip probability.

    # reasoning — synthesise the factors into a decision argument (required)
        Written AFTER the factors. This is your conclusion, not a summary.
        Identify which factor(s) tip the decision and explain how opposing signals were weighed.
        Example: "Despite dangerous temperature and wind, fuel moisture of 0.75 (D4) from recent
        rain makes ignition unlikely — the moisture environment overrides the atmospheric danger."
        If all factors align, state that clearly. If they conflict, name the override and why.

</Instructions>

